INT. VINEYARD TASTING ROOM - LATE AFTERNOON

Rustic beams. A long wooden bar. Racks of bottles lit by amber bulbs. Through the open French doors, rows of vines stretch toward the hills.

CORINNE BASSETT-OYELARAN (30s, linen dress, hair in a loose knot) swirls a glass of red and studies the color. At her elbow, her fiancé WILLIAM "WILL" TRANH (30s, rolled sleeves, sunburned nose) pretends to sniff the bouquet. And behind the bar, the vintner, MR. AUGUSTE LEFEBVRE-MORROW (60s, apron, silver hair), polishes a glass.

AUGUSTE
Notes of cherry, perhaps a little leather.

CORINNE
(lying)
I can taste the leather.

WILL
(lying)
So can I.

AUGUSTE
Excellent. You have a fine palate.

Corinne sips. Will sips. Auguste pours a sliver of white into a third glass and slides it across.

CORINNE
Auguste, this is lovely.

AUGUSTE
Please. Call me Gus.

WILL
Gus, do you ever sell your reserve?

AUGUSTE
Never.

CORINNE
Never?

AUGUSTE
My late wife made it. I open a bottle on our anniversary. That is all.

Will and Corinne exchange a look. Will sets down his glass. Corinne touches Auguste's wrist, gently, and he smiles, embarrassed.

AUGUSTE (CONT'D)
But you two are getting married, yes?

CORINNE
In September.

AUGUSTE
Then you must have one.

WILL
We couldn't.

AUGUSTE
I insist.

He ducks under the bar and returns with a dusty bottle, its label hand-lettered in faded ink. He wipes it with a cloth and reads aloud.

AUGUSTE (CONT'D)
Nineteen ninety-eight. The year we met.

CORINNE
Gus, this is too much.

AUGUSTE
It is exactly enough.

He pops the cork. Pours three small glasses. Hands one to each of them. They raise the glasses.

AUGUSTE (CONT'D)
To love.

CORINNE
To love.

WILL
To love, and to leather.

They laugh, clink, drink. The late sun slants through the doors and turns the wine to garnet.

CORINNE
Oh.

WILL
What?

CORINNE
It tastes like a memory.

AUGUSTE
That is the secret. A good wine always does.
